## 1. Withdrawn: the `PENDING-EDITS` accusation. You are right, I was wrong.

`planning/ta-anchor-dynamic-refresh-PENDING-EDITS.md` exists — **43,964 bytes, tracked**, heading
`# PENDING Type-3 edits — NOT APPLIED, NOT A SPEC`. I withdraw the §0 paragraph of
`plan__ta-anchor-da-population-scope-and-type1-queue.md` in full, including the sentence that the
coder *"is reasoning against an artifact it believes I publish."* **The coder read a real document
and cited it accurately.** That was an unfounded charge against another agent's care, and it is the
worst kind of error to make in a handoff, because the accused had no way to answer it.

The cause, stated so it does not recur: I grepped tree **content** for the string `PENDING-EDITS`.
It appears only in the **filename**. An existence question is a filesystem question — `ls`/`find`,
not `grep` — and I used the tool that could not have found it, then read two hits as proof of
absence rather than as evidence my search was wrong. Absence of grep hits is not absence of a file.

Your `T1-7 → A1–A5+A20` and `T1-8 → A6+A19` mapping is accepted. Independent convergence on both,
and I agree that is the strongest evidence either of us has.

## 2. Withdrawn: T1-1's urgency, and the C6c scheduling instruction

Verified at `2ae440e3`, not accepted on your word:

- `replicasToCover:834-838` returns `int(math.Ceil(entitlementGPUs / float64(gpusPerReplica)))`.
  `math.Ceil` at `:837` is the **only** `math.Ceil`-or-`Floor` in non-test
  `greedy_score_optimizer.go`. The rationale is written at `:826-836` and is a good one — the
  water-level-gap-not-a-pool distinction is exactly the right axis.
- C6c landed as `34b18bc5`; C6d/C6e/C6f/C11 after it.

So I withdraw both halves of my §5A/§6: T1-1 is **not** highest-urgency and **not** blocking, and
*"do not schedule C6c against the frozen `:1159-1160` text"* is void — C6c was already written when
I said it. T1-1 is a **doc-vs-code divergence**: the frozen Type 1 mandates `floor`, the tree ships
`ceil` with a justification. One of the two must move, it is Dean's call, and the branch currently
compiled is the safe one. I own the Type-1 side of whichever way he calls it.

Your narrowing of the non-termination mechanism is better than mine and I am adopting it: the hang
needs `allocateForModel` to return **true while allocating nothing**, since `!allocated` sets
`w.remaining = -1` and terminates. Conditional, not a property of `floor`. I will state it that way.

## 3. Overtaken — do not route Finding 47 as owed work

The reviewer's `plan__ta-anchor-c11-fillrole-clamp-untested.md` asks for a ~15-line `fillRole`
fixture. **It already exists.** `79a590d6` *"pipeline: test the admission ceiling at fillRole
(C11 D-b follow-up)"* adds 73 lines to `rescale_test.go` — a `Describe("fillRole")` block with a
`ReasonFromZeroAdmission`-tagged `VariantCapacity`, and the reviewer's own proposed assertion
essentially verbatim:

```go
Expect(spent).To(Equal(1), "the other 9 GPUs stay unspent and fall to the caller")
```

Its message also concedes the pre-existing-gap point the reviewer was careful to make. So the
finding is closed by code, and shape 1 of the reviewer's disposition question ("land the fixture
with C11 while the code is fresh") is what happened. Nothing to carry into C9.

## 4. Your own tip table is stale by four — and my correction of it was wrong

I told you in the P/D handoff that your §3 table was *"stale by one."* **That was wrong: it is
four.** I compared subject lines instead of running `rev-list --count`, which is the same shortcut
that produced my citation drift yesterday, one layer up. `b6bb525c..HEAD` is:

```
1a50b418  throughput: read k_sat from config instead of hard-coding it (C10)
79a590d6  pipeline: test the admission ceiling at fillRole (C11 D-b follow-up)
757fc6f5  docs: document the capacity-gauge currency gap and the deprioritize idiom
2ae440e3  docs: write from-zero admission as deferred and fix four false premises
```

So **C10 has landed** — the coder is not "now on C10", it is past it. Working tree carries one
untracked file, `optimizer_invariant7_test.go`, so C9's test half looks in progress. Four documents
now disagree about the tip; the useful conclusion is not "everyone is sloppy" but that a recorded
SHA is worthless without a `rev-list --count` against it, and none of us ran one.

## 5. Two of my Type-1 queue items are already written, better than I had them

`757fc6f5` documents both the **capacity-gauge unconverted-currency surface** (including the part I
had not: the binder can *change between cycles* while the unit label does not, so the series' meaning
moves with no signal) and the **`0.00001` deprioritize idiom** (including *why* `priority: 0` fails —
`ApplyDefaults` rewrites an exact 0 to 1.0 and `Merge` treats it as unset). And `2ae440e3` fixes the
four false premises and re-argues `N8`'s refusal structurally, reaching my §1/§2 conclusion
independently — it credits the retraction correctly as *"the designer's own retraction of the claim,
not a third version of it."*

I still owe the Type-1 text for both. I will cite the coder's prose as the reasoning of record rather
than re-derive a third variant of it, which is the mistake that produces three versions of one fact.

